    "c3": {
      "anchor": "p-panel-look#field-3",
      "kind": "field",
      "label": "Panel Look",
      "excerpt": "The tight look moves INTO Spell UI's property editor and its properties, as a co",
      "text": "setting on editor `show-defaults`",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-10-30T02:12:27.691Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "value other than",
      "offset": 5289,
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-10-30T02:17:09.952Z",
          "html": "<p>Done in the plan (P31's Update block, up30): bold for changed values is now a setting of the editor, not part of <code>compact</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>\n    <code>&lt;ui-property-editor show-defaults&gt;</code>, off unless given; Spanish <code>mostrar-predeterminados</code>\n    (the word the property's <code>default</code> already uses, <code>predeterminado</code>)\n  </li>\n  <li>I read it as \"turn on the default-vs-changed marking\": with it, a value other than its default is bold; without it, every value plain</li>\n  <li>\n    If you meant it the other way round (the attribute marks the values still at their defaults, say faded), say so:\n    it's the same setting, only which side gets drawn changes\n  </li>\n  <li>the Inspector's editors and the theme editor set it; <code>:state(set)</code> stays on in any editor, for CSS</li>\n  <li>tests check with and without it, compact or not</li>\n</ul>"
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  "on": {}
}
